1
00:00:08,148 --> 00:00:14,090
In this video, we cover the concept
of encoding into frames.

2
00:00:15,310 --> 00:00:18,910
To start, let's revisit what a link is.

3
00:00:18,910 --> 00:00:24,198
So in between network devices is
some physical transmission medium.

4
00:00:24,198 --> 00:00:27,290
Whether it's a wire or it's wirelessly.

5
00:00:28,670 --> 00:00:33,742
A link is the abstraction on
top of that physical medium,

6
00:00:33,742 --> 00:00:36,605
which enables communication.

7
00:00:36,605 --> 00:00:41,929
So the first component of that
abstraction that's solved by the link

8
00:00:41,929 --> 00:00:46,626
layer is how to encode the signals
such that they make sense.

9
00:00:46,626 --> 00:00:50,304
That they indicate who
the data is meant for and

10
00:00:50,304 --> 00:00:53,240
how to actually interpret the data.

11
00:00:55,290 --> 00:00:59,797
So, let's assume that
you can transmit 1s and

12
00:00:59,797 --> 00:01:04,990
0s through whatever
medium that's being used.

13
00:01:04,990 --> 00:01:07,908
So the link layer is responsible for

14
00:01:07,908 --> 00:01:14,370
helping make sense of this series of
1s and 0s, which needs structure.

15
00:01:14,370 --> 00:01:18,190
At the link layer,
this structure is called a frame.

16
00:01:19,890 --> 00:01:25,201
So a frame is broken down
into three parts a header,

17
00:01:25,201 --> 00:01:28,050
a trailer and the payload.

18
00:01:29,350 --> 00:01:33,766
The payload is the actual data
that the sender wants to exchange

19
00:01:33,766 --> 00:01:35,190
with the receiver.

20
00:01:36,730 --> 00:01:39,830
The header and trailer are extra metadata,

21
00:01:39,830 --> 00:01:43,610
which provides information
about the frame.

22
00:01:43,610 --> 00:01:49,215
Each of the header and
trailer can be broken down into fields.

23
00:01:49,215 --> 00:01:51,728
So let's get a little
bit more concrete here.

24
00:01:51,728 --> 00:01:53,936
Let's talk about Ethernet,

25
00:01:53,936 --> 00:01:59,490
which is pretty ubiquitous as
the main wired link layer protocol.

26
00:01:59,490 --> 00:02:03,978
So shown at the bottom is
the frame format for Ethernet.

27
00:02:03,978 --> 00:02:08,847
The dest, field for
destination is 48 bits and

28
00:02:08,847 --> 00:02:13,270
it indicates who the frame is meant for.

29
00:02:13,270 --> 00:02:18,812
It allows a receiver, namely the NIC or
Network interface card,

30
00:02:18,812 --> 00:02:23,060
to filter messages that
aren't destined for it.

31
00:02:24,150 --> 00:02:32,090
The source tells who the sender of
the frame is, and this is useful.

32
00:02:32,090 --> 00:02:38,540
So, if there's a reply message that is
needed, we can know who to reply to.

33
00:02:39,950 --> 00:02:47,790
The type field is 16 bits and tells
the receiver what the payload actually is.

34
00:02:47,790 --> 00:02:52,459
So on the right is a chart of
some of the different values of

35
00:02:52,459 --> 00:02:56,420
the type field in Ethernet and
what they mean.

36
00:02:56,420 --> 00:03:01,757
So, for example,
if the type field is x0800,

37
00:03:01,757 --> 00:03:06,226
that means what's in the payload should be

38
00:03:06,226 --> 00:03:11,330
interpreted by the receiver
as an IPV4 packet.

39
00:03:12,790 --> 00:03:17,112
So finally, the FCS field,
which stands for

40
00:03:17,112 --> 00:03:22,478
Frame Check Sequence,
provides some error handling.

41
00:03:22,478 --> 00:03:29,190
And here it's shown at the right hand side
of the frame because it's the trailer.

42
00:03:29,190 --> 00:03:35,437
And it's put as the trailer because
those are the last bits to be received.

43
00:03:35,437 --> 00:03:40,773
And so the network card can then
check if there's any errors

44
00:03:40,773 --> 00:03:47,082
that occurred because it has all
the bits before it already received.

45
00:03:49,650 --> 00:03:55,510
Now, we said that the source and
destination addresses were 48 bits.

46
00:03:55,510 --> 00:04:02,630
So in Ethernet these are called MAC
addresses for media access control.

47
00:04:02,630 --> 00:04:09,745
And it's typically displayed in hex with
colons separating each eight bits or

48
00:04:09,745 --> 00:04:14,570
two hex characters,
as shown in the example.

49
00:04:14,570 --> 00:04:19,821
So historically,
these were assigned as part of the global

50
00:04:19,821 --> 00:04:25,712
manufacturing process and
they were meant to be globally unique.

51
00:04:25,712 --> 00:04:29,605
So a vendor would get
assigned the upper bits and

52
00:04:29,605 --> 00:04:35,542
then when it's manufacturing devices
it would choose the lower bits and

53
00:04:35,542 --> 00:04:40,325
make them unique for
each of the devices it manufactured.

54
00:04:40,325 --> 00:04:46,535
With Virtualization this isn't really the
case as much anymore as the virtualization

55
00:04:46,535 --> 00:04:51,821
software will commonly just use any
addresses for the virtual machines.

56
00:04:55,130 --> 00:05:02,973
One special address to know
about is the broadcast address,

57
00:05:02,973 --> 00:05:10,523
which is all 48 bits are 1s or
all hex characters are Fs.

58
00:05:10,523 --> 00:05:15,004
So this is set as a destination
address in the frame and

59
00:05:15,004 --> 00:05:21,790
it indicates that this frame is meant to
be received by all nodes in this network.

60
00:05:24,450 --> 00:05:27,567
Now we discussed Ethernet in detail, but

61
00:05:27,567 --> 00:05:31,418
there are many different
link layer protocols.

62
00:05:31,418 --> 00:05:34,474
So each will have its own frame format.

63
00:05:34,474 --> 00:05:41,251
So shown here are on the left is ATM and
802.11,

64
00:05:41,251 --> 00:05:46,377
also known as WiFi is shown on the right.

65
00:05:46,377 --> 00:05:51,838
The key takeaway is that some
standard defines the frame format so

66
00:05:51,838 --> 00:05:57,110
everyone that's using it can know
how to speak to one another.

67
00:06:00,202 --> 00:06:06,768
Thanks, this is the end and
we will see you in the next video.